              {
                "annotations": [],
                "text": "Analyse the transformation rule from the training examples:\n\n1. **Input Grid Structure:**\n   - The input has a cross made of a specific color $C$ (e.g. 7, 3, 7) that divides the grid into 4 quadrants.\n   - The cross consists of a single vertical line and a single horizontal line of color $C$.\n   - In the top-left quadrant (above and to the left of the cross), there is a pattern of dots of another color (e.g., 8, 1, 9).\n   - Let the quadrant size be $N \\times N$.\n     - In Example 1: input is $7 \\times 7$, cross is at row 3, col 3. Top-left quadrant is $3 \\times 3$. Output is $6 \\times 6 = 2N \\times 2N$.\n     - In Example 2: input is $13 \\times 13$, cross is at row 6, col 6. Top-left quadrant is $6 \\times 6$. Output is $12 \\times 12 = 2N \\times 2N$.\n     - In Example 3: input is $7 \\times 7$, cross is at row 3, col 3. Top-left quadrant is $3 \\times 3$. Output is $6 \\times 6 = 2N \\times 2N$.\n\n2. **Output Grid Construction:**\n   - The output size is $2N \\times 2N$.\n   - The cross lines are removed.\n   - The pattern in the top-left quadrant is replicated across all 4 quadrants using reflection / 4-fold symmetry:\n     - Top-left quadrant ($N \\times N$) contains the original pattern, but colored with the cross color $C$ instead of the original dot color!\n     - Top-right quadrant is the horizontal reflection (mirror horizontally).\n     - Bottom-left quadrant is the vertical reflection (mirror vertically).\n     - Bottom-right quadrant is mirrored both horizontally and vertically.\n   - In short, the top-left $N \\times N$ quadrant (with non-zero pixels replaced by color $C$) is reflected across horizontal and vertical axes to form a $2N \\times 2N$ grid with full bilateral symmetry (D4 symmetry or Klein 4-group reflections).\n\nReady for the test input!",
